    "evidence_text": "When a sentence is given and encoded into a vector by the encoder, the decoders are trained to predict the next sentence and the previous sentence.\n\nInferSent  instead pre-trains the model in a manner of supervised learning.\n\npre-training task to learn the sentence representation.\n\nNote that these in-adjacent sentences will introduce much more training noise to the model.\n\nIn summary, when A is given, the pre-training example for each label is constructed as follows: IsNext: Choosing the adjacent following sentence as B. IsPrev: Choosing the adjacent previous sentence as B. IsNextInadj: Choosing the in-adjacent following sentence as B. There is a sentence between A and B. IsPrevInadj: Choosing the in-adjacent previous sentence as B. There is a sentence between A and B. DiffDoc: Drawing B randomly from a different document.\n\nWe used AdamW  optimizer with a learning rate of 1e-4, a $\\beta _1$ of\n\n0.9, a $\\beta _2$ of 0.999 and a L2 weight decay rate of $0.01$.\n\nWe explore three method settings for comparison.\n\nThe predictions were obtained by evaluating the training checkpoint with the best validation performance.",
